# Masa/Mura SES URLs: /kontakt/ -> /index.cfm/kontakt/
#
# Masa emits its links without the index.cfm segment (and 301s
# /index.cfm/kontakt -> /kontakt/), so every SES URL 404s unless something in
# front of Tomcat rewrites for us. In dev that is the lucee-manager nginx; the
# container has no reverse proxy, hence this file.
#
# Three Tomcat specifics below differ from Apache httpd, and all three are
# load-bearing:
#
#  1. RewriteCond patterns run through Matcher.matches(), so they must match the
#     WHOLE test string. An Apache-style partial pattern like `!\.cfm(/|$)`
#     silently never excludes anything - hence the ^...$ anchors everywhere.
#  2. -f/-d resolve through the matched context's WebResourceRoot rather than the
#     filesystem, so the operand is a request path, not the Apache idiom
#     %{DOCUMENT_ROOT}%{REQUEST_URI}.
#  3. %{REQUEST_URI} is percent-encoded, %{REQUEST_PATH} is decoded. The resource
#     tests must use the decoded one or every asset with a space or an umlaut in
#     its name misses its own file and gets handed to Masa as a 404.
#
# The index.cfm guard is load-bearing too: /index.cfm/kontakt/ is not a real
# file, so without it the valve rewrites it to /index.cfm/index.cfm/kontakt/.
# /health is a second Tomcat context and this valve is host-wide. Everything
# that exists on disk - /core/, /themes/..., /sites/..., /admin/, / - is left
# alone by the -f/-d conditions.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI}  !^/(index\.cfm|health|rest)(/.*)?$
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI}  !^.*\.(cfm|cfml|cfc)(/.*)?$
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_PATH} !-f
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_PATH} !-d
RewriteRule ^/(.*)$ /index.cfm/$1 [QSA,L]
